WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.078 --> 00:00:08.261
Every day, one and a half billion people turn to face an ancient oasis on the edge of

2
00:00:08.281 --> 00:00:10.182
the Arabian Desert and bow in prayer.

3
00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:15.745
It is the destination of almost four million visitors every year, and in the last month of the

4
00:00:15.805 --> 00:00:21.088
Islamic calendar, its population swells with two million pilgrims on a sacred journey called the hajj.

5
00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:27.012
They are coming to see Mecca, the birthplace of the prophet Muhammad and the religion he founded, Islam.

6
00:00:27.776 --> 00:00:32.857
This city is located in the western region of Saudi Arabia known as the Hejaz, a narrow stretch

7
00:00:32.897 --> 00:00:36.858
of land that runs 1,400 kilometers along the coast of the Red Sea.

8
00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:41.220
Mecca was a holy place long before the birth of Muhammad in 570 AD.

9
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.821
Since ancient times, the city had been a gathering point for traders following caravan routes between the Mediterranean,

10
00:00:48.242 --> 00:00:50.082
East Africa, and South Asia.

11
00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:55.283
Many of these travelers would stop at a small shrine known as the Kaaba, built at the site

12
00:00:55.303 --> 00:00:57.084
of a natural spring called the Zamzam.

13
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.444
This spring made Mecca a desirable spot for traders to rest and conduct business, but it had a

14
00:01:02.484 --> 00:01:04.086
particular significance to Muslims.

15
00:01:05.207 --> 00:01:10.291
According to Islamic tradition, the spring was discovered by Hagar, the second wife of Abraham and mother of

16
00:01:10.371 --> 00:01:15.456
Ishmael. Abraham is considered the forefather of Judaism, Christianity, and Islam.

17
00:01:16.136 --> 00:01:20.220
He built the Kaaba at the spot where the angel Gabriel commanded him to rebuild the shrine first

18
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.121
erected by Adam.

19
00:01:22.202 --> 00:01:26.946
When Hagar could not find water for Ishmael, the angel Gabriel touched the ground with his wing and

20
00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.307
created the Zamzam spring.

21
00:01:29.568 --> 00:01:34.511
By the time Muhammad was born, several hundred different gods and idols were being worshipped at the Kaaba

22
00:01:34.551 --> 00:01:40.175
shrine. Judaism and Christianity, monotheistic religions, were also practiced at the Kaaba shrine.

23
00:01:41.096 --> 00:01:47.040
Islam holds that Muhammad, a caravan trader from Mecca, received revelations from God in a nearby cave.

24
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.043
He declared that the many gods worshipped in the city were false and that Allah was the one

25
00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:53.104
and only true God.

26
00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:58.670
Muslims consider Muhammad to be the last in a series of prophets that includes the ancient prophets of

27
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:04.033
the Jewish tradition and Jesus Christ, and that Allah is the same God worshipped by these religions.

28
00:02:05.153 --> 00:02:10.335
The ruling tribe of Mecca, however, did not accept the prophet's message, leading to conflict between the believers

29
00:02:10.595 --> 00:02:11.436
and the nonbelievers.

30
00:02:12.336 --> 00:02:17.258
Muhammad was forced to leave Mecca for the city of Medina, 330 kilometers to the north.

31
00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:20.540
In Medina, Muhammad attracted a large following.

32
00:02:21.312 --> 00:02:26.616
The Meccan rulers, however, continued to persecute converts to Islam and attacked Medina several times.

33
00:02:27.377 --> 00:02:33.901
In retaliation, Muhammad and his followers repeatedly attacked caravans going to and from Mecca in order to undermine

34
00:02:33.941 --> 00:02:35.422
the commerce of the people of Mecca.

35
00:02:36.343 --> 00:02:41.927
Finally, in 630 AD, the Muslims launched an assault on Mecca and took control of the holy city.

36
00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:48.192
Muhammad destroyed the statues of the pagan gods in the Kaaba shrine, dedicated Mecca to the worship of

37
00:02:48.312 --> 00:02:53.386
Allah, and established the pilgrimage to Mecca as a fundamental element of the religion of Islam.

38
00:02:54.807 --> 00:03:01.431
Islam spread quickly in the centuries after Muhammad, as Muslim warriors conquered the Middle East, North Africa, Spain,

39
00:03:01.591 --> 00:03:02.571
and other parts of Europe.

40
00:03:03.432 --> 00:03:09.015
From farther and farther away, the Muslim faithful made the arduous journey to Mecca by boat, by camel,

41
00:03:09.235 --> 00:03:09.815
and on foot.

42
00:03:10.716 --> 00:03:16.819
Today, Islam is practiced from Indonesia and China to West Africa and Spain, and Mecca sees pilgrims from

43
00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:17.920
every corner of the world.

44
00:03:18.844 --> 00:03:23.228
Every Muslim who is healthy enough and has the financial means is expected to make the hajj to

45
00:03:23.268 --> 00:03:24.269
Mecca at least once.

46
00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:30.134
Pilgrims usually arrive at Jidda, a port city near Mecca, and travel to Mecca in groups.

47
00:03:30.935 --> 00:03:36.199
Before their arrival in Mecca, they stop to change into the ihram, a garment consisting of seamless white

48
00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:39.722
cloth that symbolizes their state of purity for the rest of the journey.

49
00:03:41.184 --> 00:03:46.984
On the first day of the hajj, pilgrims perform a tawaf (circling the Kaaba seven times), then walk

50
00:03:47.024 --> 00:03:52.629
between Mount Safa and Mount Marwa seven times in memory of Hagar's search for water, and then set

51
00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:56.812
out from Mecca to nearby Mina, a city where they camp and prepare for the rest of their

52
00:03:56.852 --> 00:04:03.017
journey. When they reach the Plain of Arafat, where Muhammad gave his famous farewell sermon, they participate in

53
00:04:03.037 --> 00:04:05.239
the "standing," the central rite of the hajj.

54
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.903
The pilgrims pray for forgiveness and spend hours in individual worship.

55
00:04:10.844 --> 00:04:15.968
At an open area called Muzdalifah, between the Plain of Arafat and Mina, pilgrims collect stones.

56
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.884
Then, when they reach Mina, they throw these stones at pillars that represent the temptations of the devil

57
00:04:22.164 --> 00:04:24.205
in a rite called the "stoning of the jamarat."

58
00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:30.429
After sacrificing a sheep or goat, the pilgrims go to Mecca where they perform a final tawaf and

59
00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:31.850
drink from the Zamzam spring.

60
00:04:32.771 --> 00:04:38.575
After the hajj, many visitors journey north to Medina, Muhammad's home for many years and the place where

61
00:04:38.615 --> 00:04:39.476
his tomb is located.

62
00:04:40.736 --> 00:04:45.980
The Kaaba today is a cubical structure 16 meters tall that is draped in black silk embroidered in

63
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.082
gold with verses from the Koran, the sacred book of Islam.

64
00:04:49.942 --> 00:04:53.585
The Kaaba stands in an open courtyard at the center of the Masjid al-Haram.

65
00:04:54.345 --> 00:04:59.448
Seven tall minarets surround this mosque, and 64 gates allow entrance to its vast courtyard.

66
00:05:00.349 --> 00:05:05.933
The Masjid al-Haram is the largest mosque in the world and is capable of holding 1.2 million people

67
00:05:05.993 --> 00:05:10.536
comfortably. The scale of the hajj is an awe-inspiring vision of human devotion.

68
00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.163
In one short week, over two million pilgrims visit the city and nearby sites.

69
00:05:16.064 --> 00:05:20.848
Tent cities are set up at Mina, and the Plain of Arafat becomes a sea of white-robed pilgrims.

70
00:05:22.089 --> 00:05:26.232
The government of Saudi Arabia now regulates the number of visas it issues for the hajj.

71
00:05:27.213 --> 00:05:32.117
At other times in the year, many Muslims make a lesser pilgrimage, called the umrah, which keeps the

72
00:05:32.137 --> 00:05:32.658
city busy.

73
00:05:33.538 --> 00:05:39.523
The government has spent billions of dollars improving infrastructure in the area, for example, building roads and expanding

74
00:05:39.563 --> 00:05:44.649
mosques, providing health services and housing to visitors, and trying to ensure public safety.

75
00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:52.352
Even though Mecca is not open to traditional tourism—people of faiths other than Islam are forbidden to enter—pilgrims

76
00:05:52.372 --> 00:05:58.133
spend over thirty billion dollars each year visiting the holy sites of Islam, and Mecca's economy depends almost

77
00:05:58.213 --> 00:05:59.594
exclusively on this income.

78
00:06:00.934 --> 00:06:05.096
When they return to their homes, Muslims who have made the journey to Mecca are granted a special

79
00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:11.931
title—hajji. But Mecca's importance to Islam transcends its role as a religious center and the destination of the

80
00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:18.775
hajj. A modern pilgrim flying home will notice that the airplane's onboard screens not only display information about

81
00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:23.537
the plane's speed, altitude, and estimated arrival time, but also an arrow pointing to Mecca.

82
00:06:24.358 --> 00:06:29.120
Five times every day, when Muslims unroll their prayer mats to pray, no matter where they may be,

83
00:06:29.381 --> 00:06:30.801
they first turn to face Mecca.

84
00:06:31.562 --> 00:06:36.660
By this act, Muslims all over the world unite around the holy city that is the spiritual center

85
00:06:36.761 --> 00:06:37.303
of their faith.
